language ESSENCE' 1.0
$ finding_celebrities: an Essence' model from DCP Rosetta.
$
$ Given a list of people at a party and, for each person, the list of people they know at the party, find the celebrities at the party. A celebrity is a person that everybody at the party knows but that only knows other celebrities. At least one celebrity is present at the party. For each person, say whether they are a celebrity or not.
$
$ Save this file as model.eprime. Download `finding_celebrities.json` and `dcp_convert_essence.py` from the
$ problem's page:
$   https://dcp-bench.github.io/DCP-Rosetta/problems/finding_celebrities.html
$ Convert the instances once, then run the first one with Savile Row 1.11 (it needs Java); the
$ solution is written to `finding_celebrities_1.param.solution`:
$   python dcp_convert_essence.py finding_celebrities.json
$   savilerow model.eprime finding_celebrities_1.param -run-solver

$ graph_dim1: the number of people at the party (derived from the data; the graph is square)
given graph_dim1 : int
$ graph[i,j]: 1 when person i knows person j, indices 1-based
given graph : matrix indexed by [int(1..graph_dim1), int(1..graph_dim1)] of int(0..1)

$ celebrities[i]: person i is a celebrity
find celebrities : matrix indexed by [int(1..graph_dim1)] of bool

branching on [celebrities]

such that
$ at least one celebrity is present at the party
exists i : int(1..graph_dim1) . celebrities[i],
$ a person is a celebrity exactly when everybody else at the party knows them and they know only other celebrities
forAll i : int(1..graph_dim1) . (celebrities[i] <-> (forAll j : int(1..graph_dim1) . (j != i -> (graph[j,i] = 1 /\ (graph[i,j] = 1 -> celebrities[j])))))
